#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded Between the X-Axis and a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "61af7bef-751d-5c35-9e5f-cdb649957e68" :question/math-academy-id "e-3887" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^3$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1432/Source/Images/ba25ac7d1486034bfea03158e8407dae.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}x^{3} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3} &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n0 \\in \\left[-1,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]\n$$\n\n, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0$.\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [-1,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\nA_1 = -\\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFor\n\n$$\nx \\in\\left[0,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]\n$$\n\n, we have\n\n$$\nA_2 = \\int_{0}^{3/2}\\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThe total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 =-\\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{3/2} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFinally, we carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= - ∫_{-1}^{0}\\frac{x^{3}}{2}dx + ∫_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{x^{3}}{2}dx \\\\\n&= - [\\frac{x^{4}}{8}]_{-1}^{0} + [\\frac{x^{4}}{8}]_{0}^{3/2} \\\\\n&= - [0 - \\frac{1}{8}] + [\\frac{81}{128} - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} + \\frac{81}{128} \\\\\n&= \\frac{97}{128}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18cd52a9-1524-5099-b1d8-858345328bad" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral That Gives the Area Bounded by a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "19c30733-019b-5e03-90c8-988114cb3135" :question/math-academy-id "e-3888" :question/problem "The total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=x^3-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=2$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a$?\n$A = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{a} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{a}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1432/Source/Images/57a34212c16bef10ef454f07479d2053.png)" :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=x^3-1$. So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{3} - 1 \\\\\n(0) &= x^{3} - 1 \\\\\nx^{3} &= 1 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1 \\in [-2,2]$, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x={\\color{blue}{1}}$.\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x={\\color{blue}{1}}$ to $x=2$.\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nand the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{\\color{blue}1} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{\\color{blue}1}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110407 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d89ba41-6d24-548a-bd97-66830b752ba9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded By a Curve When It Crosses the X-Axis Several Times"}] :question/id #uuid "64490be9-a9f9-5580-9ac3-99637c273100" :question/math-academy-id "e-3889" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=x^3-9x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - 9x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 9) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 3)(x + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$, $x=-3$, and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $0 \\in [-3,3]$, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0$.\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1432/Source/Images/2a563bcdf5a67c69214ca4fcde498ea4.png)\n\nFor $x \\in [-3,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= ∫_{a}^{b}y(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-3}^{0}(x^{3} - 9x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-3}^{0}x^{3}dx - 9∫_{-3}^{0}xdx \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{4}}{4} \\mid _{-3}^{0} - \\frac{9x^{2}}{2} \\mid _{-3}^{0} \\\\\n&= [0 - ((-3)^{4})/(4)] - 9[0 - ((-3)^{2})/(2)] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{81}{4} + \\frac{81}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{81}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{3} \\left(x^3-9x\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{2} &= - ∫_{0}^{3}(x^{3} - 9x)dx \\\\\n&= - ∫_{0}^{3}x^{3}dx + 9∫_{0}^{3}xdx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{x^{4}}{4} \\mid _{0}^{3} + \\frac{9x^{2}}{2} \\mid _{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= - [((3)^{4})/(4) - 0] + 9[((3)^{2})/(2) - 0] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{81}{4} + \\frac{81}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{81}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 = \\dfrac{81}{4} + \\dfrac{81}{4} = \\dfrac{81}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded Between the X-Axis and a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be2ba077-c564-5979-a7ea-7e0411dc88ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109578} {:answer/id #uuid "3752b994-dd2a-582c-8319-db5d5e456305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109579} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9bdd53-49f8-5d93-a91b-12fe06cb3c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109580} {:answer/id #uuid "4b58bd2b-b55a-5f59-8606-b3be5f21e87f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109581} {:answer/id #uuid "595bfdd5-f78a-546d-8a6e-54f34d8cd103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be2ba077-c564-5979-a7ea-7e0411dc88ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109578} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec1813f-ab90-5df1-9a11-88dd1b272b79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109577}] :question/id #uuid "6bd86f6d-7571-5622-89bf-f474680288ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-54475" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1432/Source/Images/q-54475.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y = \\frac{1}{2}x^{3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x =-1$ to $x = 1$, as shown above." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
